        <div id="review-container"></div>
        
        <div class="long-ans-box">
            <h3>Section B: Descriptive Answers (From Answer Key)</h3>
            
            <div class="ans-item">
                <strong>Q2. Explain (a): "If in thine my life thou waste, Thou art the best of mee."</strong><br>
                This line is from John Donne's poem "Sweetest Love, I Do Not Goe". The poet says to his beloved that if she wastes her life in sorrow after his death, it can't be true that she loves him. He means that she should not cry and waste her life in his memory.
            </div>
            
            <div class="ans-item">
                <strong>Q2. Explain (b): "Until they think warm days will never cease, For Summer has o'er-brimm'd their clammy cells."</strong><br>
                These lines are from John Keats' poem "Ode to Autumn". The poet describes the abundance of autumn. The warmth of autumn days gives the illusion that warm days will never end. Summer has filled the honeycombs (clammy cells) to the brim.
            </div>

            <div class="ans-item">
                <strong>Q5. Short Answers (Selected from Key):</strong><br>
                <strong>(a) What did the Fire Hymn say to the speaker?</strong> The Fire-Hymn said to the speaker that he had forgiven him and now it has taken an oath to overlook and pardon him for the sin he committed.<br><br>
                <strong>(c) How does Keats personify autumn in the poem?</strong> Autumn is personified as one conspiring with the sun to yield a rich, ripened harvest. Also, it is personified as having hair that is "soft lifted by the winnowing wind".<br><br>
                <strong>(g) Write a short note on William Shakespeare.</strong> William Shakespeare was an English poet, playwright, and actor, born on 26 April 1564 in Stratford upon Avon. He is regarded as the greatest writer in the English language and is nicknamed the Bard of Avon. He wrote about 39 plays, 154 sonnets, and two long narrative poems. He wrote famous works like Hamlet, Othello, King Lear, and Macbeth.<br><br>
                <strong>(h) What are the important elements of a drama?</strong> The important elements of a drama are Plot, Character, Dialogue, Gestures, Scenic effects, Music, and Soliloquy.
            </div>
        </div>

        <button onclick="location.reload()" style="margin-top: 30px;">Take Test Again</button>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. Rank List Section -->
    <div id="rank-section" class="section">
        <h2>Admin: List</h2>
        <div id="password-prompt">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 15px;">Enter Password to view List</p>
            <input type="password" id="adminPassword" placeholder="Enter Password">
            <button onclick="checkPassword()">View List</button>
        </div>
        <div id="rank-list-data" style="display: none;">
            <table class="rank-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Rank</th>
                        <th>Student Name</th>
                        <th>Score</th>
                        <th>Date</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody id="rank-tbody">
                    <!-- Data goes here -->
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <button onclick="location.reload()" style="margin-top: 30px;">Back to Home</button>
        </div>
    </div>

</div>

